      "norm_3_and_6_exclusion_proof": "In the displayed section basis, ker(G mod 3) is generated by the last coordinate, whose raw vector is (-23,1^23), congruent to (1^24) mod 3. Thus every vector in the pairing radical has all raw coordinates congruent mod 3. Raw norm 3 means square sum 24: residue 0 is impossible, while residue +/-1 forces all coordinates +/-1 with one common sign, contradicting coordinate sum zero. Raw norms 4 and 5 have square sums 32 and 40. In a nonzero residue class, common odd parity makes every coordinate equal to the least representative +/-1 unless the square sum is already at least 48, contradicting sum zero; common even parity has square sum at least 96. Residue zero is impossible modulo 9. For raw norm 6, square sum 48. Residue 0 is again impossible. Writing a residue-1 coordinate as 1+3k, the zero-sum and square-sum equations give sum(k)=-8 and sum(k^2)=8, hence exactly eight coordinates are -2 and sixteen are 1; this violates the common coordinate parity of every odd-Leech vector. Residue -1 is the negative case. Therefore the radical has no vector of norm at most 6 (in particular no norm 3 or 6 vector)."
